{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## UPDATE — 2722 merged, alarm prediction now live\n\nChange **2722** (\"Let the probe reach the cap scripts it runs\", `rebar-ticket: 5fb0-89ab-4466-41cc`)\nis MERGED. **Zero open Gerrit changes.** Eleven changes merged this session.\n\nThat was the blocker named in the handoff section above for the four remaining alarms\n(`container-reaper-not-active`, `container-writable-usage-high`, `var-tmp-cleanup-not-active`,\n`var-tmp-usage-high`). The prediction to check on the next sweep, stated so it is falsifiable:\n\n- The alarms clear only after autodeploy **re-materialises the probe** — merging alone is not\n  enough. Verify on the host, not from the merge.\n- When they do clear, the two heartbeats should read **1**, not **-1**. A `-1` means the three-value\n  sentinel is working but the **installer half did not take**, which is a distinguishable and useful\n  failure signature rather than an ambiguous non-recovery.\n- **Caveat:** `octahedral-auburn-sunfish` records that autodeploy's `*_PATHS` do not list the cap\n  scripts, so a cap-only change may reach no host until something else re-materialises the probe.\n  If the alarms do not clear, that is the likely reason — not a bad fix.\n\nEnvironment at this point: main green on the gating cell, Gerrit HTTP 200 in 0.27s, 4 alarms with 0\nINSUFFICIENT_DATA, no open changes."},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788670199303565001,"uuid":"3ed38855-0a3c-4dd2-8117-77e4723695fa"}